# Rust
/target/
# An agent lane that cannot use a worktree points CARGO_TARGET_DIR somewhere
# else in this tree instead, and leaves several gigabytes behind. Ignored so it
# does not bury a real stray in `git status` -- but delete them too, since
# `/target/` being ignored is exactly why the first one went unnoticed.
#
# ⚠ This rule reaches only inside the repository, and the directories that
# actually grew were **siblings** of it -- outside any .gitignore's reach and
# outside `git status`. `just janitor` is what sees those.
/target-*/
**/*.rs.bk

# Marks a worktree as a throwaway agent lane. Written by `just agent-worktree`,
# read by the two build variables at the top of the justfile.
#
# ⚠ Ignoring it is load-bearing, not tidiness: `just janitor` refuses to remove
# a worktree with untracked files, so a tracked-or-visible marker would hold
# every lane it creates forever.
/.agent-worktree

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Environment
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Windows junk
nul

# Build artifacts
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test fixtures (copyrighted game files).
#
# Everything staged under a fixture root is game content, at any depth: the
# corpus is organised `<title>/<target>/`, so a one-level pattern would miss most
# of it. The README that documents the layout is the only tracked file there.
crates/io/or-io-verify/tests/fixtures/*
!crates/io/or-io-verify/tests/fixtures/README.md

# The sibling corpus, reached from inside the tree.
#
# ⚠ Git descends into a Windows junction as though it were an ordinary
# directory, and `core.symlinks` does not change that because a junction is not
# a symlink. So `mklink /J corpus ..\openrage-corpus` -- a reasonable way to
# have the corpus on hand without copying it -- offers git every file behind
# the link as its own entry rather than one link entry, and `git add -A` stages
# them one by one. Measured on the owner's checkout: 6,964 files behind the
# junction, 593 of them carrying an extension the list below does not name and
# therefore ignored by nothing at all.
#
# This is a permanent rule for an arrangement that is expected, not a cleanup.
# Two spellings because whoever makes the junction names it, and these are the
# two names it gets. Anchored to the root, so a directory called `corpus` that
# is genuinely source stays visible wherever else in the tree it sits.
/corpus/
/openrage-corpus/

# The same content again, by extension.
#
# Not redundancy. The rules above name paths, and a path-shaped rule stops
# working the moment a crate moves -- which is exactly what happened: the
# fixture roots were `crates/format/or-io/` and `crates/format/or-meta/` until
# the V2 split relocated them, the patterns were not updated, and the next
# `git add -A` swept in 414 MB of game resources and 6,000 metadata files
# across 24 commits without anything noticing.
#
# A RAGE extension is game content by definition -- nothing in this repository
# authors one -- so following the content is more durable than following the
# directory. Whichever rule fires first, the file stays out.
#
# ⚠ The list grows by measurement, never by transcribing the engine's extension
# table. `.yed`, `.ypdb` and `.yfd` are here because a real corpus held 590 of
# them and not one rule above named any; the other extensions that table lists
# are absent because no corpus has yet been seen to carry them, and an ignore
# rule that is wider than the evidence hides files somebody wanted to see.
*.ypt
*.ytd
*.ydr
*.ydd
*.yft
*.ybn
*.yld
*.ycd
*.yed
*.ypdb
*.yfd
*.ymt
*.ymf
*.ymap
*.ytyp
*.pso
*.rpf
*.gxt2
*.fxc
*.dds

# There used to be an exception here. Two files -- a `.ydr` drawable fixture and
# the metadata schema snapshot -- were tracked from before these rules existed,
# and git does not consult .gitignore for a path it already tracks, so the list
# above did not reach them. They have been retracted from history and the
# exception is gone with them; the rules below now apply to both without
# qualification, and the provenance lint fails if either returns.

# Descriptor pack sources.
#
# A pack carries the engine's own type and field names, which is the point of
# it and the reason it is not published from here. The usual place for one is a
# private repository checked out beside this tree; this rule is for anyone who
# keeps a working copy inside it instead.
#
# Nothing in this repository needs a pack to build. See docs/direction/OR_IO_Specification.md.
/packs/

# The built artefact, wherever it lands. A `.orpack` *is* the private tier --
# it carries the engine's type and field names -- so the extension is ignored
# outright rather than only the directory it usually sits in.
#
# The directory rule is here because `just pack` used to default `--out` to a
# path relative to the shell's working directory, which put two of these in the
# repository root and then into three commits before anyone noticed.
*.orpack
/dist/

# Scratch space for staged samples, probe output and one-off scripts. Whatever
# is in here came out of a game install, so it is game content by default and
# a whole-directory rule is the only safe one.
/tmp/

# Python bytecode, from the throwaway scripts in tools/. A one-off analysis
# script is worth keeping; the cache the interpreter leaves beside it is not,
# and it reappears on every run.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Redirected command output. `cargo test > tmp_test.log` is the obvious way to
# read a long run, and one of those spent months tracked at 330 KB because
# nothing stopped it. Any `.log` at the root is scratch by definition.
/*.log

# The metadata schema snapshot.
#
# The snapshot is a machine-extracted derivative of a source tree that is not
# ours to redistribute, so neither ships. Nothing compiles one in: the editor
# and the CLI install one found at run time, and without one metadata still
# reads and writes exactly as before, and field names show as hashes. See
# crates/io/or-io/src/meta/README.md.
# By name rather than by path, for the same reason the extension list above
# exists: this file has moved once already. The stamp beside it names the
# generator that wrote it and means nothing without it.
schema_snapshot.bin
schema_snapshot.stamp

/test-files/

# `docs/` is documentation *of OpenRage* and ships. `docs/extra/` is the tier
# that documents RAGE's proprietary binary layouts, or leans on the GTA source
# tree or CodeWalker to do it, and stays local. A whole-directory rule because
# the distinction is about provenance, not file type.
/docs/extra/

# Agent harness state.
#
# ⚠ Ignoring these hides more than settings. A harness that makes its own
# `git worktree` puts it under one of them — measured: 3.93 GiB at
# `.claude/worktrees/agent-ae40727c07fa874ab`, a second full checkout of this
# repository inside the checkout. Ignored, so `git status` never mentions it;
# not ignored, so every recursive walk that does not read `.gitignore` still
# descends into it. `just janitor-dry` names any worktree outside the lane root
# and marks this one `[inside the checkout]`, which is the only report that
# finds it. A lane belongs under `../openrage-lanes/`, not here.
/.claude/
/.cursor/
/.opencode/

*-repomix-output.xml

/_bmad/
/_bmad-output/
/repomix/

# .NET build output. A tool without these rules once had 1.8 MB of Release
# artifacts committed, including a 1.16 MB PDB.
tools/cw-compare/bin/
tools/cw-compare/obj/
# A shader definition database is extracted from a game install, so it is game
# content and must never be committed. It is written wherever the operator asks
# for it, so the rule is by name rather than by path.
*.shaderdefs.json
